  "raw": "\n\nBut the state’s more northern counties will remain under a special drought declaration until Jan. 1, the U.S. Drought Monitor said Thursday.\n\nThat drought declaration affects five counties — Alpine, Mono, Sierra, Inyo and Siskiyou — where abnormally dry conditions persisted into October.\n\nThe heavy snowpack that blanketed California this winter was also necessary for an early end to state rains that fell on the drought-ravaged state in late 2017 and early 2018.\n\nDrought conditions improved in July and August but worsened in September and October, state officials said.\n\n“The wet weather in late 2017 and 2018 was welcome, especially in the Sierra Nevada mountains,” said state climatologist Daniel Swain, lead author of the Drought Monitor’s monthly report.\n\nThe stormy weather also gave California the biggest snowpack on record at the start of winter. The average snowpack statewide was nearly double",
  "serialized": "But the state’s more northern counties will remain under a special drought declaration until Jan. 1, the U.S. Drought Monitor said Thursday. That drought declaration affects five counties — Alpine, Mono, Sierra, Inyo and Siskiyou — where abnormally dry conditions persisted into October. The heavy snowpack that blanketed California this winter was also necessary for an early end to state rains that fell on the drought-ravaged state in late 2017 and early 2018. Drought conditions improved in July and August but worsened in September and October, state officials said. “The wet weather in late 2017 and 2018 was welcome, especially in the Sierra Nevada mountains,” said state climatologist Daniel Swain, lead author of the Drought Monitor’s monthly report. The stormy weather also gave California the biggest snowpack on record at the start of winter. The average snowpack statewide was nearly double",
